e over time i've  bracketed ct values of 25 and 30 just  arbitrarily so you can get  a better sense of where the medians and  the  interquartile ranges are for the pc  values  the other thing i'd point out which is  something we're generally pleased with  is how tight the data is  so the method tends to be very  reproducible um  and within a lot uh we tend to see  very good uh stability over time  um some of the data points off to the  uh right hand side of the graph where  you see a little bit more variability  um interestingly and i'll get to this in  a few graphs our volume has gone down so  there's a little bit more variability  just associated with reduced volume over  time  and that's just a factor that you just  have to keep in mind  all right the next thing i wanted to go  over was i had mentioned this  the fact that we use these extraction  controls also to  to detect possible plate rotations  um as an example uh every 3d four well  plate which is the panel you see on the  bottom  is comprised of four 96 well plates and  you see what we call  an alpha plate and a charlie plate on  the top  there is a bravo and a delta that's  obscured right now by the 384 well plate  in each of the alpha bravo charlie and  delta sets  two positive controls and a negative  control are located in different  positions in the plates  uh and where they wind up on the 3d4  well plate is shown as shown in the  bottom panel so for example the positive  control 1  on the alpha plate is in well b1 that  winds up being  in well c1 on the 3d4 wall plate based  on how we interleave them  and similarly for the charlie plate  positive control 1 on a charlie plate  is in well let's see that would be a10  and it winds up on the three-day four  wall plate in well b19  so if an alpha charlie bravo or delta  plate were  rotated uh this pattern of controls of  positive control one negative  and positive control two would appear in  defined locations  so we have an algorithm that goes and  looks to see uh  whether that pattern appears where it it  should not  appear and confirms that it does appear  where it's expected to appear so that's  part of our  quality control process there are many  ways to solve this problem this is just  one that we found  operationally uh straightforward to  implement  okay now i want to go on to talk about  pathologies of the individual  assays and amplifications themselves and  some of this data was kindly provided by  thermo fisher  one of the updates to the instructions  for use  that came out fairly recently within the  last month and a half or so  was the importance of vortexing the 3d  four wall plate  as i mentioned the extracted rna has to  be mixed very well with  the real-time pcr master mix in  probe and primer mix the density excuse  me the viscosity of the master mix  is very high and so it's very important  to vortex this very thoroughly  if you do not do that what you see  in the very early cycles of the assay as  the temperature  fluctuates the temperature itself the  convection  is actually driving mixing and so you  see  some changes in the signal in the first  few cycles  as the assay is mixed thermally  had it been mixed um through vortexing  this background in the first few cycles  would be  flat so this is something that's fairly  easy to pick up on is now and is now  part of our standard curve review  um if you look at an amplification plot  again  you would see this substantial amount of  background in the first few cycles  as evidence that the plate was not well  mixed  so package insert now says not only  vortex this very carefully but also  check the curves of every run to ensure  that  it has in fact been well mixed  all right um other examples  of irregular curves that may occur for  example if there was an inhibitor or  some other  methodological problem went wrong um the  the upper  upper left graph shows a great  amplification curve you know clear  exponential amplification meaning a nice  linear region in a log plot  um in this case of all four targets so  things working quite nicely  the graph to the right of that that says  not true pcr amplification  you see a few amplification curves that  are shaped exactly as expected and you  see others that  that rise abruptly and then have a  sharp elbow and a long drift cycle  that is not true pcr amplification it  may have something to do with irregular  background in early cycles or may have  something to do with  another unusual reaction condition  bottom left not true pcr amplification  with high background you can see these  what we call lazy curves that are rising  slowly over time  um and to the to the left you see that  the background in the first few cycles  is nearly the same level  of the of the curves during  amplification  and then bottom right you see quite a  sporadic signal almost a sawtooth  pattern uh in some of these which again  may be related to an instrument issue an  electrical noise issue  or something related to the assay but  these are not real curves  and merit further investigation before  data like that would be  would be moved forward for release  okay um so that expands on some of the  pathologies now i want to get into  um looking at large sets of data that  we've generated over the last several  months so  uh with over a hundred thousand data  points um  you know it's always interesting to look  at the the three targets that are in the  assay there's the in gene the s gene and  the orf one a b  region of the uh sars cov2 genome  and so i've plotted here um  uh sort of a two dimensional density  plot of the ct values of these various  targets  and so this would indicate for example  if the  n-gene ct  is well i'll say it this way if the s  gene ct  is slightly higher than the n gene ct at  the same  sample then it would suggest that the n  gene  is slightly more sensitive at detecting  stars cov2  than the s gene so that's this little  kick up that you see at the very end but  overall  uh you know very near linear throughout  the the dynamic range of the assay  n versus orf1 again very similar s  versus rf1  again pretty similar you might infer  from this that uh  you know all in gene is the most  sensitive or f1ab  is the next most sensitive s is is  probably the least sensitive of the  three  um but still valuable across uh across  time  um you'll also notice uh something this  struck me when i was putting this data  together that there's a bit of  vital modality you'll see there's a bit  more concentration  down here in the low ct ranges you know  so these would be we would call this  high viral load although we  would hesitate to say that a nasal swab  is an accurate way to  to capture a viral load um but certainly  within the assay you know this is a lot  of virus  uh in the ct values between sort of 15  and 20.  and then there's another peak up here in  the around  30 cts and we'll expand on that in in  the next slide  so this was 100 000 data points taken  over  uh several months if i now look  over time um i'm showing you that same  data but by week of the year so week 23  was june 1st the week of june  1st the last panel on this graph week 34  was august 19th so as you'll recall  uh and certainly within the geographies  that we serve texas and  and the southwest um you know the  pandemic was  really uh creeping back up you know post  post-memorial day and uh uh  so we believe you know the data suggests  that there were  very many high viral loads uh in these  first few weeks of june going through  all of june  and then starting in sort of uh july  uh we had a lot of people testing if you  you  might remember from the popular media uh  there was an absolute  surge in demand for testing in a few  weeks in july  and so as that surge occurred  you see this bimodality emerge uh where  we still have some very high positives  but we also have some very weak  positives represented by the  the the peaks at higher ct values up  around 30.  and in fact that trend just continues  and so now the the  majority of the peak is actually at ct  values  higher than 30 as of the week of august  19th  um to expand on that story a little bit  more what we've seen  sort of from an epidemiological  perspective uh positivity rate  at least in the southeast um  really blossomed so this graph shows you  you know by  date positivity rate uh so i've i've  drawn lines at ten percent and twenty  percent  uh starting just after july fourth you  know we  we pretty consistently went over 20  positive  uh in the samples that we were testing  and then  as we expanded into august that  positivity rate dropped back below  uh 20 percent and in fact today it's  back down below 10  um but as that positivity rate  followed that course the ct values that  we see just the average ct values these  are box plots of ct values  tended to drift up um and this might  suggest that  for example either people who were  suspicious of disease and catching it  earlier were being tested  or people who perhaps had had the  disease and now we're in late stage and  wanting to confirm  no residual nucleic acid existed we're  coming back to be tested  unfortunately as a laboratory we don't  really get to understand  every patient's clinical trajectory so i  can't easily tease those apart but the  data certainly suggests  that there were a large number of people  being tested uh perhaps late  in uh in an infectious state or after  they had already had acute disease  um the other story here of course is  that uh while we're very pleased with  the tack path  uh turnaround time and capacity  it still is only a certain amount of  capacity so  the huge demand that erupted in  june you can see that's when the  positivity rate started to spike up  and our turnaround times uh you know we  try to maintain turnaround times well  under 48 hours because that's the most  useful  turnaround time for the test is as fast  as possible  but once you're completely uh you know  the testing demand far  outstrips the uh capacity of any  laboratory and this was  every laboratory in the country was  being outstripped at the time  uh the turnaround times by necessity  increased but since then you can see  they've been quite low and stable  all right uh the final sort of  interesting clinical picture here is to  look  at as i mentioned we do antibiotics as  well so in some cases i was able to find  patients who  we had tested with the tacpath assay and  later tested  with either the igt antibody or the  total antibody  and i haven't discriminated between the  two and i'm just asking the question you  know sort of does biology work  um so this is looking at  patients who had uh positive antibody  tests  that were more than two weeks after an  initial  uh nucleic acid test that was also  positive and we're just asking the  question what do the ct values look like  and you see the ct values are broadly  distributed there's  this sort of bi-modality to it i  wouldn't put a whole lot of faith in  that because  as i recall there's not a huge amount of  data in this  but it does indicate that uh you know  people who were acutely infected at one  point with perhaps a very high ct  did develop an antibody response if i  now change the question slightly and say  show me patients who had a positive  nucleic acid test  and a later antibody result but it was  less than two weeks after so perhaps  they did not  develop fully uh an immune response  in uh between the two tests you see that  their  ct values are consistently only very  high and this suggests to us  that uh these are patients who were  tested by nucleic acid very early  uh in their infection right when they  were  had very low viral load and the time  between  the detection here and the antibody test  was too short their immune system had  not actually developed detectable levels  of antibody  so the the story here is that the you  know covid  does follow traditional infectious  disease routes and we see the  both nucleic acid antibody responses  that you would expect  all right so that's uh that's it i would  be happy to take any questions and thank  you again for the opportunity
 

